565 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(1.3.1 HISTORY)/E(1.3.1 HISTORY)/Pg 10 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

566 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The land currently within the Town was occupied by the Cayuga tribe of Native Americans, who concentrated on the banks of Catatonk Creek and used the valley floor and hills for agriculture, hunting, and shelter. The land was first deeded to Europeans in a 363,000 acre land purchase, and surveyed, split, and sold in 1793. Settlers led by Joel Smith and Isaac Judd began arriving on tracts in 1794, predominantly engaging in subsistence agriculture in the Catatonk valley\220s fertile soils. Candor\220s remoteness required residents\220 ingenuity and cooperation. Fast-flowing local creeks provided the power for a gristmill and sawmill built in 1795, and many built later. New settler arrivals created the villages of West Candor, Weltonville, Catatonk, and Hubbardtown, and the official Town of Candor was incorporated in 1811. )/E(The land currently within the Town was occupied by the Cayuga tribe of Native Americans, who concentrated on the banks of Catatonk Creek and used the valley floor and hills for agriculture, hunting, and shelter. The land was first deeded to Europeans in a 363,000 acre land purchase, and surveyed, split, and sold in 1793. Settlers led by Joel Smith and Isaac Judd began arriving on tracts in 1794, predominantly engaging in subsistence agriculture in the Catatonk valley\220s fertile soils. Candor\220s remoteness required residents\220 ingenuity and cooperation. Fast-flowing local creeks provided the power for a gristmill and sawmill built in 1795, and many built later. New settler arrivals created the villages of West Candor, Weltonville, Catatonk, and Hubbardtown, and the official Town of Candor was incorporated in 1811. )/Pg 10 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

567 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(The railroad arrived in Candor in 1854, and brought many changes by not only connecting the town to larger markets but also influencing the development of Candor's infrastructure. The railroad station and associated facilities were the first organized planning effort in the town, providing a central hub for commerce and travel. Later, a Town Planning Board was established to help balance the preservation of Candor's rural character as more planned development occurred. In the post-World War II era, the Town became somewhat of a \215bedroom community\216 as the construction of highways made it easier for residents to commute to larger cities for work while retaining the charm of rural living in Candor. )/E(The railroad arrived in Candor in 1854, and brought many changes by not only connecting the town to larger markets but also influencing the development of Candor's infrastructure. The railroad station and associated facilities were the first organized planning effort in the town, providing a central hub for commerce and travel. Later, a Town Planning Board was established to help balance the preservation of Candor's rural character as more planned development occurred. In the post-World War II era, the Town became somewhat of a \215bedroom community\216 as the construction of highways made it easier for residents to commute to larger cities for work while retaining the charm of rural living in Candor. )/Pg 10 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

568 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(In recent decades, Candor has taken steps to preserve its agricultural heritage and natural beauty. Efforts to protect open spaces, conserve farmland, and promote sustainable practices have become integral to the town's planning initiatives. The Town\220s Comprehensive Plan functions to address new challenges and opportunities within Candor, with a commitment to controlled development.)/E(In recent decades, Candor has taken steps to preserve its agricultural heritage and natural beauty. Efforts to protect open spaces, conserve farmland, and promote sustainable practices have become integral to the town's planning initiatives. The Town\220s Comprehensive Plan functions to address new challenges and opportunities within Candor, with a commitment to controlled development.)/Pg 10 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

569 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(1.3.2 VILLAGE/TOWN RELATIONSHIP & GOVERNMENT STRUCTURES)/E(1.3.2 VILLAGE/TOWN RELATIONSHIP & GOVERNMENT STRUCTURES)/Pg 10 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

570 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/T(Nested within the Town of Candor is a smaller government for the Village of Candor. The Village of Candor is .44 square miles, located near the center of the Town and makes up approximately 15% of the Town of Candor\220s total residents. Around 2009, the Village Board of Trustees launched a dissolution study in partnership with the New York State Local Government Efficiency program to determine the impacts of a potential merging of the governments of the Village of Candor and the Town of Candor. The dissolution report provided some key information about the relationship between these two entities that are critical to this Comprehensive Plan. )/E(Nested within the Town of Candor is a smaller government for the Village of Candor. The Village of Candor is .44 square miles, located near the center of the Town and makes up approximately 15% of the Town of Candor\220s total residents. Around 2009, the Village Board of Trustees launched a dissolution study in partnership with the New York State Local Government Efficiency program to determine the impacts of a potential merging of the governments of the Village of Candor and the Town of Candor. The dissolution report provided some key information about the relationship between these two entities that are critical to this Comprehensive Plan. )/Pg 10 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

571 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/ActualText(TOWN OF CANDOR - COMPREHENSIVE PLAN)/Pg 10 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

572 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/ActualText(03)/Pg 10 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

573 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(The Village of Candor\220s operations are organized into two funds: the General Fund and the Water Fund. The Water Fund is funded through rates applied specifically to customers who receive water from the local system, not through the Village property tax. In 2023, the Village used a grant to complete a water main replacement project. Through its General Fund, the Village provides other public works, such as street snow plowing, pothole repairs, blacktopping roads, and hanging holiday lights and street banners, to name a few. )/E(The Village of Candor\220s operations are organized into two funds: the General Fund and the Water Fund. The Water Fund is funded through rates applied specifically to customers who receive water from the local system, not through the Village property tax. In 2023, the Village used a grant to complete a water main replacement project. Through its General Fund, the Village provides other public works, such as street snow plowing, pothole repairs, blacktopping roads, and hanging holiday lights and street banners, to name a few. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

574 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(There is, however, some informal collaboration built into public service management between the Village and Town. The Village water system operates mostly in the Village, but it bills 36 properties in the Town just outside the Village and provides free/unmetered water to Town properties such as Town Hall and the Town Highway Department Barn facility.  Conversely, the Village stores its salt at the Town Highway facility, has access to town equipment to salt roads, and sometimes receives assistance from the Town on covering their side roads. )/E(There is, however, some informal collaboration built into public service management between the Village and Town. The Village water system operates mostly in the Village, but it bills 36 properties in the Town just outside the Village and provides free/unmetered water to Town properties such as Town Hall and the Town Highway Department Barn facility.  Conversely, the Village stores its salt at the Town Highway facility, has access to town equipment to salt roads, and sometimes receives assistance from the Town on covering their side roads. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

575 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(This arrangement makes sense as the Town is more well-equipped to maintain roads. Whereas the Village has 2.7 miles of roads to maintain, the Town has 154 miles. Similarly, the Village only has 1 full-time employee, the Superintendent of Public Works, compared to the 11 full-time employees at the Town Highway Department. For both the Town and the Village, street maintenance is the largest expenditure of the Highway / Department of Public Works budget. )/E(This arrangement makes sense as the Town is more well-equipped to maintain roads. Whereas the Village has 2.7 miles of roads to maintain, the Town has 154 miles. Similarly, the Village only has 1 full-time employee, the Superintendent of Public Works, compared to the 11 full-time employees at the Town Highway Department. For both the Town and the Village, street maintenance is the largest expenditure of the Highway / Department of Public Works budget. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

576 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(Other examples of services that are provided separately by the Town and Village include their independent court systems \(both served by the same part=time Court Clerk\) and their attorneys \(the same attorney contracted separately\). )/E(Other examples of services that are provided separately by the Town and Village include their independent court systems \(both served by the same part=time Court Clerk\) and their attorneys \(the same attorney contracted separately\). )/Pg 11 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

577 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(The New N.Y. Government reorganization and Citizen Empowerment Act in 2010 was passed in order to encourage the dissolution of Villages in the state. This law was motivated by the understanding that New York has too many levels of governments that provide services \(counties, cities, towns, and villages\) and that this contributes to higher property taxes. Indeed, the Village dissolution study found that taxes would be lowered by an average of $380 in the Village of Candor if the dissolution occurred, and Town taxes outside the Village would only need to rise by $9.  Proponents of the Law argued that villages are already part of the towns in which they are located\204they pay taxes and vote in elections of both the village and the town while \(typically\) having much smaller populations than their towns. However, a report from the Rockefeller Institute finds that many villages reject referendums to dissolve due to concerns related to their community identity/pride, the importance of local buildings and symbols, appreciation of local public servants, and the other community celebrations organized at the village level.  In Candor, residents rejected )/E(The New N.Y. Government reorganization and Citizen Empowerment Act in 2010 was passed in order to encourage the dissolution of Villages in the state. This law was motivated by the understanding that New York has too many levels of governments that provide services \(counties, cities, towns, and villages\) and that this contributes to higher property taxes. Indeed, the Village dissolution study found that taxes would be lowered by an average of $380 in the Village of Candor if the dissolution occurred, and Town taxes outside the Village would only need to rise by $9.  Proponents of the Law argued that villages are already part of the towns in which they are located\204they pay taxes and vote in elections of both the village and the town while \(typically\) having much smaller populations than their towns. However, a report from the Rockefeller Institute finds that many villages reject referendums to dissolve due to concerns related to their community identity/pride, the importance of local buildings and symbols, appreciation of local public servants, and the other community celebrations organized at the village level.  In Candor, residents rejected )/Pg 11 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

