1138 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/T(The Town of Candor's geology was broadly shaped by extensive glaciation and erosion acting on a bedrock of Devonian rocks formed 360 million years ago. Beginning 2 million years ago, glaciation eroded pre-existing features and deposited a deep mix of glacial till across the landscape, forming drumlins, loess deposits, and deep river valleys. As the glaciers melted 20,000 years ago, The Town of Candor was covered by numerous lakes, rivers, and deltas that redistributed clays and silts from the uplands into valleys, forming deep and rich alluvial soils. Continuing upland erosion and the emergence of Candor\220s creeks produced the contemporary landscape, composed of north-south oriented rocky upland ridges flanking wide river valleys. Existing local bedrocks include siltstone, glacial till mixes, sandstone, and shale.  Candor features slope classes ranging from flat to 35%, with extreme slopes present along valley walls and surrounding hills. Development is generally discouraged by the State\220s Department of Environmental Conservation on slopes over 15% to preserve soil, control erosion, and limit stormwater runoff. )/E(The Town of Candor's geology was broadly shaped by extensive glaciation and erosion acting on a bedrock of Devonian rocks formed 360 million years ago. Beginning 2 million years ago, glaciation eroded pre-existing features and deposited a deep mix of glacial till across the landscape, forming drumlins, loess deposits, and deep river valleys. As the glaciers melted 20,000 years ago, The Town of Candor was covered by numerous lakes, rivers, and deltas that redistributed clays and silts from the uplands into valleys, forming deep and rich alluvial soils. Continuing upland erosion and the emergence of Candor\220s creeks produced the contemporary landscape, composed of north-south oriented rocky upland ridges flanking wide river valleys. Existing local bedrocks include siltstone, glacial till mixes, sandstone, and shale.  Candor features slope classes ranging from flat to 35%, with extreme slopes present along valley walls and surrounding hills. Development is generally discouraged by the State\220s Department of Environmental Conservation on slopes over 15% to preserve soil, control erosion, and limit stormwater runoff. )/Pg 52 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1139 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H4/T(9.1.2 SOILS)/E(9.1.2 SOILS)/Pg 52 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

1140 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H5/T(The Town of Candor hosts a large variety of soils, consisting of alluvial and lacustrine silty and gravelly loams in creek valleys and silty clays and loams in the surrounding uplands. With the limited exceptions of the poorly-drained Volusia and Chippewa series, most soils are well-drained to excessively well-drained. Upland soils tend to be shallower and more weathered than valley soils given higher erosion rates. In addition to individual soil qualities, land use practices should take into account considerable local slopes and resulting drainage patterns.  Predominant soil series in Candor\220s creek valleys include the Middlebury, Howard, and Chenango soil series. All three are considered prime agricultural soils, generally featuring silty clay or gravelly loam textures with good drainage and nutrient retention. Depth to the water table is often relatively shallow. Howards and Chenangos feature silicate clay layers or accumulation, with deep and well-defined structure. Much of Candor\220s agricultural land is concentrated on extensive Howard series along the Catatonk and Willseyville creeks.  Common upland soils include the Lordstown, Mardin, and Volusia series. Mardin is considered a prime agricultural soil, with silty loam texture and good drainage, while )/E(The Town of Candor hosts a large variety of soils, consisting of alluvial and lacustrine silty and gravelly loams in creek valleys and silty clays and loams in the surrounding uplands. With the limited exceptions of the poorly-drained Volusia and Chippewa series, most soils are well-drained to excessively well-drained. Upland soils tend to be shallower and more weathered than valley soils given higher erosion rates. In addition to individual soil qualities, land use practices should take into account considerable local slopes and resulting drainage patterns.  Predominant soil series in Candor\220s creek valleys include the Middlebury, Howard, and Chenango soil series. All three are considered prime agricultural soils, generally featuring silty clay or gravelly loam textures with good drainage and nutrient retention. Depth to the water table is often relatively shallow. Howards and Chenangos feature silicate clay layers or accumulation, with deep and well-defined structure. Much of Candor\220s agricultural land is concentrated on extensive Howard series along the Catatonk and Willseyville creeks.  Common upland soils include the Lordstown, Mardin, and Volusia series. Mardin is considered a prime agricultural soil, with silty loam texture and good drainage, while )/Pg 52 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

