endobj

1149 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Candor\220s waterbody resources include four principal creeks, 650 acres of permanent and perennial wetlands, and two significant aquifers. All creeks flow )/E(Candor\220s waterbody resources include four principal creeks, 650 acres of permanent and perennial wetlands, and two significant aquifers. All creeks flow )/Pg 53 0 R/K[6]>>
endobj

1150 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/ActualText(Tioga County Agricultural and Farmland Protection Plan, 2015)/Pg 53 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

1151 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(generally north-south through broad floodplain valleys with outlets into the Susquehanna River and Chesapeake Bay Watershed. Principle creeks include Pipe Creek, Catatonk Creek, Doolittle Creek, and the West Branch Owego Creek. Catatonk Creek features two dams in Candor Village which create ponds with potential recreational opportunities.  All of Candor\220s creeks are classified as Class C by New York State, indicating water quality sufficient for fishing and primary recreational water contact. Survey respondents indicated a desire to better use Candor\220s creeks for flood control and recreation, including boating, fishing, and swimming. Catatonk Creek has a municipally-owned frontage in Candor Village which could provide public creek access. No levees or other flood control infrastructure currently exist in the Town.  Catatonk Creek features extensive wetlands in Willseyville and Candor, providing essential habitat for local and migratory birds, mammals, and amphibians. )/E(generally north-south through broad floodplain valleys with outlets into the Susquehanna River and Chesapeake Bay Watershed. Principle creeks include Pipe Creek, Catatonk Creek, Doolittle Creek, and the West Branch Owego Creek. Catatonk Creek features two dams in Candor Village which create ponds with potential recreational opportunities.  All of Candor\220s creeks are classified as Class C by New York State, indicating water quality sufficient for fishing and primary recreational water contact. Survey respondents indicated a desire to better use Candor\220s creeks for flood control and recreation, including boating, fishing, and swimming. Catatonk Creek has a municipally-owned frontage in Candor Village which could provide public creek access. No levees or other flood control infrastructure currently exist in the Town.  Catatonk Creek features extensive wetlands in Willseyville and Candor, providing essential habitat for local and migratory birds, mammals, and amphibians. )/Pg 53 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

