1154 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(9.1.5 FLOOD ZONES)/E(9.1.5 FLOOD ZONES)/Pg 54 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

1155 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Candor\220s flood zones are important resources which provide fertile agricultural soils, critical wildlife habitat, and strategic flooding mitigation opportunities. Much of Candor\220s most intensely developed area is concentrated along flood zones, including  parts of Candor Village, Willseyville, and Weltonville. Candor\220s most important agricultural land is often directly in flood zones and benefits from a history of soil additions.  Candor experiences relatively frequent flooding, with significant floods in 1998, 2011, and 2018. 2011\220s flooding was particularly destructive, with the Catatonk and Owego Creeks causing damage to buildings and infrastructure in Candor, Willseyville, and West Newark. Candor\220s Upper Dam, the Elementary School, and several bridges were damaged, with some repairs taking years to fund and complete. Survey respondents have mentioned the threat of future flooding as an ongoing concern.  The relative frequency of flooding in Candor makes the strategic use of its floodplains essential. Tioga County\220s agricultural plan calls for the limitation of new development in flood zones and to use them as natural forested, wetland, or agricultural flood absorption buffers.  To regulate flood zone development, Candor\220s Town government established a Local Law subjecting flood zone developments to project review by the planning board and banning several development types in regulatory floodways. Infrastructure and buildings in flood zones are required to be hardened against flood damage or elevated above the established flood zone. )/E(Candor\220s flood zones are important resources which provide fertile agricultural soils, critical wildlife habitat, and strategic flooding mitigation opportunities. Much of Candor\220s most intensely developed area is concentrated along flood zones, including  parts of Candor Village, Willseyville, and Weltonville. Candor\220s most important agricultural land is often directly in flood zones and benefits from a history of soil additions.  Candor experiences relatively frequent flooding, with significant floods in 1998, 2011, and 2018. 2011\220s flooding was particularly destructive, with the Catatonk and Owego Creeks causing damage to buildings and infrastructure in Candor, Willseyville, and West Newark. Candor\220s Upper Dam, the Elementary School, and several bridges were damaged, with some repairs taking years to fund and complete. Survey respondents have mentioned the threat of future flooding as an ongoing concern.  The relative frequency of flooding in Candor makes the strategic use of its floodplains essential. Tioga County\220s agricultural plan calls for the limitation of new development in flood zones and to use them as natural forested, wetland, or agricultural flood absorption buffers.  To regulate flood zone development, Candor\220s Town government established a Local Law subjecting flood zone developments to project review by the planning board and banning several development types in regulatory floodways. Infrastructure and buildings in flood zones are required to be hardened against flood damage or elevated above the established flood zone. )/Pg 54 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

