1161 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(9.1.6 GROUNDWATER AND WATER QUALITY)/E(9.1.6 GROUNDWATER AND WATER QUALITY)/Pg 55 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

1162 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Candor\220s groundwater resources are protected by several local and state regulations. Candor\220s Well Hed Protection Critical Environmental Area \(CEA\), passed by the Town government in 2013, restricts development linked with industrial contaminants including shale drilling, heavy industry, and garbage dumping in a portion of Candor Village. The CEA protects a concentrated essential aquifer recharge area along Catatonk Creek in Candor\220s most densely developed area. State environmental regulations support enforcement and monitoring of Candor\220s CEA.  Other impacts to groundwater quality may be caused by hydraulic fracturing, oil and gas drilling, and establishment of garbage dumps. Fracking is currently banned by New York State law, and municipal governments\220 right to ban drilling within their borders was upheld by a State Superior Court Ruling. Importing of garbage to deposit in local dumps is banned by Section 4.2 of Candor\220s Disposal of Garbage town law. )/E(Candor\220s groundwater resources are protected by several local and state regulations. Candor\220s Well Hed Protection Critical Environmental Area \(CEA\), passed by the Town government in 2013, restricts development linked with industrial contaminants including shale drilling, heavy industry, and garbage dumping in a portion of Candor Village. The CEA protects a concentrated essential aquifer recharge area along Catatonk Creek in Candor\220s most densely developed area. State environmental regulations support enforcement and monitoring of Candor\220s CEA.  Other impacts to groundwater quality may be caused by hydraulic fracturing, oil and gas drilling, and establishment of garbage dumps. Fracking is currently banned by New York State law, and municipal governments\220 right to ban drilling within their borders was upheld by a State Superior Court Ruling. Importing of garbage to deposit in local dumps is banned by Section 4.2 of Candor\220s Disposal of Garbage town law. )/Pg 55 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

1163 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(9.1.7 UNIQUE NATURAL AREAS)/E(9.1.7 UNIQUE NATURAL AREAS)/Pg 55 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

1164 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(The Town of Candor\220s landscape hosts several unique natural features. Creek valleys, ridges, and uplands are generally oriented southeast, defined by the primary Catatonk, Pipe, West Branch Owego, and Doolittle Creeks. Each creek defines a wide, flat floodplain composed of deep and well-structured soils. The entire Town is part of the Susquehanna-Chesapeake Bay watershed.  650 acres of permanent and perennial wetlands are concentrated along Catatonk Creek, providing essential habitat for numerous avian species, amphibians and reptiles, and large and small mammals. Candor features several unique \217elevated wetlands\220 along its upland ridgelines which attract migrating birds and local species. )/E(The Town of Candor\220s landscape hosts several unique natural features. Creek valleys, ridges, and uplands are generally oriented southeast, defined by the primary Catatonk, Pipe, West Branch Owego, and Doolittle Creeks. Each creek defines a wide, flat floodplain composed of deep and well-structured soils. The entire Town is part of the Susquehanna-Chesapeake Bay watershed.  650 acres of permanent and perennial wetlands are concentrated along Catatonk Creek, providing essential habitat for numerous avian species, amphibians and reptiles, and large and small mammals. Candor features several unique \217elevated wetlands\220 along its upland ridgelines which attract migrating birds and local species. )/Pg 55 0 R/K[3]>>
endobj

1165 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(9.1.8 SOLAR DEVELOPMENT)/E(9.1.8 SOLAR DEVELOPMENT)/Pg 55 0 R/K[4]>>
endobj

1166 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H2/T(Rural areas have recently been influenced by a wave of solar development, especially in areas close to transmission lines and large energy markets. Four commercial solar projects have been built in the Town of Candor on long-term leased agricultural land. Solar projects are subject to restrictions stated in the Town\220s Site Plan Review Law, including maintaining appropriate setbacks, site fencing, and maintenance. Plans for project decommissioning and site remediation are furthermore required.  Though opinions on solar projects vary, Candor\220s residents are sensitive to the impacts solar development has on the Town\220s rural character, landscapes, and natural resources. While they can provide supplemental income for landowners and contribute renewable energy to the local grid, solar projects generally are constructed on agricultural land that constitute some of Candor\220s most important natural resources. Limited information is available on the effectiveness of facility decommissioning, economic feasibility of returning land to cultivation, and the )/E(Rural areas have recently been influenced by a wave of solar development, especially in areas close to transmission lines and large energy markets. Four commercial solar projects have been built in the Town of Candor on long-term leased agricultural land. Solar projects are subject to restrictions stated in the Town\220s Site Plan Review Law, including maintaining appropriate setbacks, site fencing, and maintenance. Plans for project decommissioning and site remediation are furthermore required.  Though opinions on solar projects vary, Candor\220s residents are sensitive to the impacts solar development has on the Town\220s rural character, landscapes, and natural resources. While they can provide supplemental income for landowners and contribute renewable energy to the local grid, solar projects generally are constructed on agricultural land that constitute some of Candor\220s most important natural resources. Limited information is available on the effectiveness of facility decommissioning, economic feasibility of returning land to cultivation, and the )/Pg 55 0 R/K[5]>>
endobj

